        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          In summer, a ceiling fan should spin counterclockwise when you stand underneath and look up, at medium or high speed, so it pushes a column of air straight down onto you. In winter, flip it to clockwise and run it on the lowest speed, so it draws air up and spills the warm layer at the ceiling down the walls without a draft. That is the whole rule. The rest of this guide is about the parts people actually get stuck on: telling which way the blades are really turning, finding the switch, and knowing when the winter setting simply is not worth the bother.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: Which Way a Ceiling Fan Should Spin
          </h2>

          <p>
            Stand directly under the fan and look up. In summer the blades should be turning counterclockwise, the opposite of a clock hand. In winter they should be turning clockwise, and the fan should be on its lowest speed. The U.S. Department of Energy gives exactly this advice in its Energy Saver guidance: counterclockwise for summer, clockwise on low for winter to move warm air from the ceiling down into the living space.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is blade pitch. Every ceiling fan blade is mounted at an angle, typically somewhere between 10 and 15 degrees on a decent fan. Spinning one way, that angle scoops air and drives it downward. Spinning the other way, the same angle pulls air up toward the ceiling. Reversing the motor does not change the blades; it changes which face of the angle leads, and therefore which way the air goes.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one honest caveat. The counterclockwise-for-summer rule describes how the overwhelming majority of fans sold in North America are built, but direction is really a proxy for airflow. A small number of fans, mostly imported or unusual designs, have blades pitched the other way. That is why the test that never lies is the one you do with your hand: in summer mode you should feel a firm breeze standing under the fan on high, and in winter mode you should feel almost nothing.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Season</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Direction (looking up)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Speed</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">What you should feel</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Summer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Counterclockwise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium to high</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">A steady breeze directly below the fan</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Winter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Clockwise</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lowest setting</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Little or no breeze; air moves along the walls</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Spring and fall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Either, or off</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Run it only when someone is in the room</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If you only remember one thing, remember the speed half of the winter rule. Plenty of people flip the switch to clockwise and leave the fan on high, which pushes air hard enough to bounce off the walls and come back down as a cool draft. Winter mode on high often feels colder than winter mode on low, which defeats the point entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="summer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Summer: Counterclockwise for the Wind-Chill Effect
          </h2>

          <p>
            In summer mode the fan builds a downdraft, a column of moving air directly underneath it that spreads out across the floor. That moving air does not lower the room temperature. What it does is strip away the thin layer of warm, humid air that sits against your skin and speed up the evaporation of sweat. Your body reads that as cooler, the same way a breeze on a hot day feels cooler than still air at the same temperature.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Department of Energy puts a number on the effect: if you use air conditioning, a ceiling fan lets you raise the thermostat setting about 4 degrees Fahrenheit with no reduction in comfort. That is the figure most guides quote, and it comes from the DOE's own Energy Saver pages. The 4 degrees is a comfort equivalence, not a guarantee. It assumes you are sitting or lying within the fan's airflow and that the fan is moving a meaningful amount of air.
          </p>

          <p>
            Speed matters more in summer than people expect. Airflow falls off sharply on the lower speeds, and the felt cooling drops off quickly at the edges of the airflow. For a bedroom at night, medium is usually the sweet spot between breeze and noise. For a living room on a hot afternoon, run it on high and sit under it. If high is too loud to live with, that is a motor problem, and a quieter DC-motor fan from our list of the <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">best ceiling fans with lights</a> is the real fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            Humidity changes how well any of this works. The cooling you feel from a fan is mostly evaporative, so it works best in dry air and loses punch as relative humidity climbs. In a muggy room, especially a below-grade space, a fan can feel like it is just pushing damp air around. The EPA recommends keeping indoor humidity between 30 and 50 percent, and if a finished basement is sitting well above that, a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier</a> will do more for comfort than any fan direction setting.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SUMMER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Counterclockwise, medium to high, and only when someone is in the room.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A fan cools the people under it, not the air. Running it in an empty room just adds a little motor heat and spends electricity on nothing.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="winter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Winter: Clockwise on Low to Push Warm Air Down
          </h2>

          <p>
            Warm air rises. In a heated room, a layer of warmer air collects against the ceiling while the air at seat height stays cooler, a pattern called stratification. In winter mode, the fan reverses and gently draws air upward in the middle of the room. That pushes the warm layer at the ceiling outward, down the walls and back into the space where people actually are, without blowing anything directly at them.
          </p>

          <p>
            The lowest speed is not a suggestion. The goal is to mix the air without creating a breeze, because any breeze on your skin produces exactly the wind-chill effect you wanted in July and do not want in January. On most three-speed AC fans, low is the only usable winter setting. On a DC fan with six speeds, start on speed one and go up only if you can confirm the room is mixing better without anyone feeling a draft.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter mode earns its keep in rooms with tall ceilings and a strong heat source. A two-story great room, a vaulted living room with a wood stove, or a loft space heated by a single ducted register can have a noticeably warmer layer at the ceiling than at the sofa. In those rooms, slow mixing brings comfort back to seat level and can let you set the thermostat a little lower. The Department of Energy recommends the setting for exactly this reason, though it does not publish a single savings figure for winter use the way it does for summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            One practical point: if your fan has been running in one direction all summer, the first reversal is a good moment for a quick check. Turn it off, tighten the blade screws and the canopy screws, and wipe the dust off the blades, which builds up unevenly and can throw the balance off. If the fan shakes after you reverse it, our guide to <a href="/why-is-my-ceiling-fan-wobbling/">why a ceiling fan wobbles</a> walks through the fixes in order of likelihood.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="check" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How to Check Which Way Your Fan Is Spinning
          </h2>

          <p>
            Watching the blades sounds easy, and it is, as long as the fan is on its lowest speed. At high speed the blades blur and the eye can be fooled into seeing them turn the wrong way, the same illusion that makes wagon wheels appear to spin backward in films. Set the fan to low, stand directly beneath it, look straight up, and follow one blade with your eyes. If it moves the opposite way a clock hand would, the fan is in counterclockwise mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            The airflow test is more reliable and settles any doubt. Switch the fan to high and stand underneath it for ten seconds. A firm, obvious breeze on your face and shoulders means the fan is pushing air down, which is summer mode. If you feel very little directly under the fan but can detect air moving near the walls, it is pulling air up, which is winter mode. A strip of tissue held at arm's length above your head will flutter downward in summer mode and lift toward the blades in winter mode.
          </p>

          <p>
            You can also read the blades themselves. Look at a stationary blade from the side and note which edge sits higher. In summer mode, the higher edge should be the one leading as the blade turns, which scoops air downward. In winter mode, the lower edge leads, and air is lifted. This is the method to use when the fan is off and you want to know what a switch position will do before you turn it on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What if neither direction produces a breeze?</h3>

          <p>
            If the fan feels weak in both directions, direction is not your problem. The fan is either too small for the room, mounted too close to the ceiling to draw air, or running slower than it should because of a failing capacitor on an AC motor. Our guide on <a href="/what-size-ceiling-fan-do-i-need/">what size ceiling fan you need</a> covers blade span, ceiling clearance and CFM, which are the three numbers that decide whether a fan can produce a usable breeze at all.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="switch" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where the Direction Switch Is on Your Fan
          </h2>

          <p>
            On most standard AC-motor fans, the reverse control is a small slide switch on the motor housing. It usually sits on the side of the housing just above the light kit or the switch cup, sometimes hidden behind the blades. It is typically a two-position slider, often unlabeled, and occasionally marked with arrows or the words summer and winter. Budget fans with pull chains almost always use this kind of switch, including the Honeywell and Westinghouse budget picks in our pillar guide.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always turn the fan off and let it stop completely before moving the switch. Flipping it while the blades are turning forces the motor to reverse against its own momentum, which strains the windings and can strip the switch. Use a sturdy step ladder rather than a chair, and do it with the power off at the wall if the switch sits close to the light kit wiring.
          </p>

          <p>
            DC-motor fans generally have no switch on the housing at all. Reverse lives on the handheld remote or wall control, usually as a dedicated button marked with circular arrows or the letters REV or R. On some remotes the button has to be held for a few seconds, and on others it only works while the fan is already running, so check the manual if a quick press seems to do nothing. Smart fans add a third route: the manufacturer's app or a voice assistant, and on models such as the Big Ass Fans Haiku line, direction is just another setting in the app.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fan type</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where reverse lives</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Watch out for</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AC motor, pull chain</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Slide switch on motor housing</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Must stop the fan before switching</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">AC motor, with remote</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Usually still the housing switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Some remotes add a reverse button; many do not</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">DC motor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Button on remote or wall control</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">May need a long press or the fan running</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smart fan</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">App, voice, or remote</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Firmware updates sometimes move the setting</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If your fan has no switch, no reverse button and no app, it may simply not reverse. That is common on some hugger fans, some decorative and very small fans, and older units. Shopping for a replacement, check the spec sheet for a reverse function, and if you want to change direction without climbing a ladder twice a year, a DC fan with remote reverse is the practical upgrade. Our tested <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fan picks</a> note which models reverse from the remote and which still use a housing switch.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pointless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. When Winter Mode Is Pointless
          </h2>

          <p>
            Winter mode is often oversold. In a typical bedroom or home office with an eight-foot ceiling, there just is not much warm air trapped up there. The room is short enough that normal convection, the heating system's own air movement and people walking around already keep the temperature fairly even from floor to ceiling. Running a fan clockwise in that room mostly spends a few watts and, if it is any faster than low, adds a slight draft.
          </p>

          <p>
            Small rooms are the second case. When a fan nearly fills the ceiling of a 10-by-10 room, the upward air hits the ceiling and walls almost immediately and comes back down as a breeze. There is no gentle wraparound effect in a room that size. If you can feel the fan in winter mode in a small room, turn it off; you are making the room feel cooler, not warmer.
          </p>

          <p>
            Forced-air systems with ceiling registers are the third. If your heat already blows out of vents in the ceiling at speed, the supply air is doing the mixing for you. Winter mode adds little. It is far more useful with baseboard heat, radiators, a wood or pellet stove, or a ducted system with floor registers in a tall room, where warm air leaves the heat source and rises straight up with nothing to bring it down.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is an easy way to find out whether your room needs it. Put an inexpensive digital thermometer on top of a tall bookcase or door frame, close to the ceiling, and another at seat height, then compare them after the heat has been running for an hour. If the difference is only a degree or two, winter mode has little to recover and you can leave the fan off. If the ceiling is several degrees warmer, slow clockwise mixing is worth doing, and you will usually feel the difference at the sofa within half an hour.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WORTH IT vs. NOT</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Tall ceilings and point heat sources: yes. Standard eight-foot bedrooms: rarely.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Vaulted rooms, lofts, and rooms with a stove or fireplace benefit most. Small rooms, low ceilings and ceiling-register forced air benefit least, and a fan on anything above low makes them feel colder.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="savings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Fan Direction Actually Saves, and What It Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            The summer savings are real but conditional. A ceiling fan draws somewhere between roughly 10 watts for an efficient DC motor on a middle speed and around 70 to 90 watts for an older AC fan on high. A central air conditioner draws thousands of watts while the compressor runs. If the fan lets you raise the thermostat by the DOE's 4 degrees, the air conditioner runs less and the trade is overwhelmingly in your favor. If you leave the thermostat where it was and simply add the fan, you have only added cost.
          </p>

          <p>
            That is the core point people miss: fans cool people, not rooms. A fan spinning in an empty room does nothing useful, and its motor actually adds a small amount of heat. The Department of Energy is blunt about it and recommends turning ceiling fans off when you leave a room. Occupancy-sensing smart fans exist precisely to automate that, and in a household where everyone forgets, they are one of the few smart features that pays for itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            To put the fan side in perspective, a 50-watt fan running eight hours a day uses 0.4 kilowatt-hours, which works out to a handful of cents a day at typical U.S. residential electricity rates. Cutting your air conditioner's runtime by even a modest amount saves far more than that. Winter savings are harder to pin down, depend entirely on how stratified the room is, and are close to zero in the standard rooms described in the previous section, so treat them as a comfort benefit first and a savings benefit second.
          </p>

          <p>
            If what you really want is a cooler house rather than a cooler person, fan direction is the wrong lever. In dry climates with cool nights, a <a href="/best-whole-house-fan/">whole-house fan</a> pulls outdoor air through open windows and pushes hot air out through the attic, which genuinely lowers the indoor temperature and can replace hours of air conditioning. A ceiling fan only rearranges the air already in the room. The two work well together: the whole-house fan flushes heat in the evening, and ceiling fans keep people comfortable through the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if your current fan cannot produce a breeze worth raising the thermostat for, that is the moment to replace it rather than fiddle with settings. Look for a fan sized to the room, a DC motor if it runs in a bedroom, and a reverse control you can reach. Our hands-on <a href="/best-ceiling-fans-with-lights/">ceiling fans with lights buying guide</a> ranks the eight we would actually install, from budget remote fans to wet-rated patio models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running winter mode on high</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Clockwise only works as a heat-mixer at low speed. On high, the air hits the ceiling, races down the walls and comes back across the floor as a cool draft. If you can feel the fan in winter, slow it down or switch it off. A breeze is exactly what you are trying to avoid in January.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Flipping the switch while the fan is spinning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Moving the housing slide switch while the blades are turning forces the motor to reverse under load, which strains the windings and can damage the switch itself. Turn the fan off, wait until the blades stop completely, then slide the switch. It takes thirty extra seconds and protects a part that is fiddly to replace.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the fan running in empty rooms</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A ceiling fan does not lower the air temperature, so running it with nobody underneath it is pure electricity spent on nothing, plus a little motor heat. The savings come only when the fan lets you raise the thermostat. Turn fans off as you leave a room, or buy one with occupancy sensing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting your eyes at high speed</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                At high speed the blades blur, and the eye can easily read the rotation backward. Check direction on the lowest speed, or skip the visual check and use the airflow test. A firm breeze directly underneath on high means summer mode; little or nothing underneath means the fan is in winter mode.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting direction to fix an undersized fan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If a fan feels weak in both directions, reversing it will not help. The usual cause is a blade span too small for the room or blades mounted too close to the ceiling to draw air. Measure the room and check the fan's CFM before blaming the setting, then size the replacement properly.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way should a ceiling fan turn in summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Counterclockwise when you stand under it and look up, on medium or high speed. That pitch pushes air straight down, creating a breeze that speeds evaporation from your skin. The Department of Energy says this lets you raise the thermostat about 4 degrees Fahrenheit with no loss of comfort, provided you actually raise it and sit within the airflow.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which way should a ceiling fan turn in winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clockwise when you look up, on the lowest speed the fan offers. The fan gently draws air upward in the middle of the room, pushing the warm layer at the ceiling out and down the walls. Keep it slow enough that nobody feels a breeze, because any draft makes a room feel cooler, which is the opposite of the goal.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know which direction my ceiling fan is spinning?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Set it to low, stand underneath, and follow one blade. If it moves opposite to a clock hand, it is counterclockwise. For a definitive answer, switch to high and stand under it: a firm breeze on your face means summer mode, while little airflow directly beneath means winter mode. A strip of tissue overhead shows the same thing.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where is the reverse switch on a ceiling fan?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On most AC-motor fans it is a small slide switch on the motor housing, usually just above the light kit or switch cup. DC-motor fans normally reverse from a button on the remote or wall control, often marked with circular arrows. Smart fans also reverse from the app. Some huggers and older fans have no reverse function at all.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does reversing a ceiling fan really save money?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In summer, yes, but only if you raise the thermostat while the fan runs; otherwise you are adding cost. In winter, savings depend on how much warm air collects at the ceiling. In tall rooms with a stove or baseboard heat the benefit can be noticeable. In standard eight-foot rooms it is close to nothing, so treat it as a comfort tweak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I run my ceiling fan in winter with low ceilings?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Rooms with eight-foot ceilings, especially small ones, have little warm air trapped overhead, and the upward airflow bounces back as a draft almost immediately. Compare a thermometer near the ceiling with one at seat height. If they differ by only a degree or two, leave the fan off for the winter and save the electricity.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do all ceiling fans spin counterclockwise for summer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The vast majority sold in North America do, because of how their blades are pitched, but a small number of designs are built the other way. Direction is really shorthand for airflow. Trust the breeze test over the rule: whichever setting produces a strong downdraft under the fan on high is summer mode, whatever it looks like.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

